<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemy of Mauretania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 40 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemy of Mauretania was the last king of Mauretania, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii">Cleopatra Selene</a> and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Cleopatra. He ruled from about 23 CE as a Roman client king until the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> summoned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and had him executed in 40 CE. A revolt led by his freedman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedemon">Aedemon</a> followed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> divided his kingdom into two provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-of-mauretania.jpg" alt="Ptolemy of Mauretania" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy_of_Mauretania_Louvre_Ma1887.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ptolemy was probably born between 13 and 9 BCE, the child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a> of Numidia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-selene-ii">Cleopatra Selene II</a>, who had been appointed rulers of Mauretania by the emperor Augustus in 25 BCE. Their court at Caesarea promoted Greek and Egyptian culture through art, building and a royal library. His mother gave him the name Ptolemy, as was traditional for male members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic dynasty</a>, rather than a Numidian or Roman one. He was probably educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where he was honoured with a statue at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">Gymnasium</a> of Ptolemy, and he probably held Roman citizenship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>From 17 CE Juba II faced a revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacfarinas">Tacfarinas</a>, a Gaetulian who had once served as a Roman auxiliary, and by 21 CE Ptolemy was co-regent. When Juba died, Ptolemy became sole ruler. He assisted the Roman proconsul of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Publius Cornelius Dolabella, in the campaign in which Tacfarinas was killed at Augea. The Senate named him a friend and ally of the Roman people and in 24 CE sent him an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> sceptre, a gold crown, a curule seat and a toga picta, all of which appear on his coins. Duane W. Roller regarded the war as a demonstration of his competence, while Anthony A. Barrett suggested that he contributed little.</p><p>Mauretania prospered under him by exporting grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, garum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">Tyrian purple</a> dye and luxury woods to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, along with animals for spectacles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colosseum">Colosseum</a>. Unlike his scholarly father, he was reputed to be an immature ruler controlled by members of his court. Near the end of his reign he began to act independently of Rome and issued gold coins, which he may not have been permitted to do.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In late 39 or early 40 CE Caligula summoned Ptolemy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> he was meant to receive honours but was arrested instead and later executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> claimed that the emperor envied his wealth, and Suetonius that his purple attire drew admiration in the amphitheatre. Modern historians suggest that Caligula saw him as a political threat, or that he was suspected in the conspiracy of Gnaeus Cornelius Lentulus Gaetulicus; Duncan Fishwick and Brent Shaw thought him almost certainly innocent.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> tried to annex the kingdom but met a rebellion led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedemon">Aedemon</a>, which destroyed Tamuda and partly destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a> and Lixus. It was suppressed shortly before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> became emperor in 41. After another rising in 42, Claudius sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a> and Gnaeus Hosidius Geta to establish Roman control, and he separated the country into Mauretania Tingitana and Mauretania Caesariensis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ptolemy of Mauretania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Claudius created Mauretania Caesariensis in 42 CE from the eastern part of the Kingdom of Mauretania, after the murder of <b>King Ptolemy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauretania-caesariensis">Mauretania Caesariensis</a></li><li><blockquote>After the execution of <b>King Ptolemy</b> and the suppression of Aedemon's revolt, Claudius divided the Kingdom of Mauretania in 42 CE.</blockquote><a href="/state/mauretania-tingitana">Mauretania Tingitana</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherchell">Cherchell</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.61,2.19&amp;year=30" aria-label="Cherchell on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4605 844 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ptolemy of Mauretania"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5055 1114h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5055 1114h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.61,2.19&amp;year=30">What was here in 30 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
